From: Ryan Hughes <ryan@heyoodle.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] soundwire: mediatek: close the interrupt gate before
 quiescing a link

mtk_sdw_enable_irq(false) cleared interrupt_enabled, then waited for a
running status work in cancel_delayed_work_sync(), and only then cleared
MCP_INT_IRQ. Until then the line stayed live, and the status work it was
waiting for re-enables the slave interrupts as it finishes. A slave
interrupt in that window reached the handler with interrupt_enabled
false, which clears the status but neither masks it nor schedules the
status work. A peripheral sitting at Dev0 raises its attach status again
as soon as it is cleared, so the handler ran back to back, printing a
line every time.

On the ASUS ProArt P14 the attach check (2 s after the link comes up)
hit that window on every boot of one kernel build. The level interrupt
is handled on CPU0, where the status work was also queued, so the work
never finished, the cancel never returned and the gate stayed open:
"[1]slave attached int IntStatus:0x80002000" about every 100 us, a
million times per boot, "ClearStatus 0x80002000" showing the flag was
off, and the boot never got past Plymouth. The same build in the
fallback entry was still storming nine minutes in. Earlier builds hit
the window too but escaped after a few hundred interrupts.

Close the gate first when disabling, so nothing can arrive once the
flag is cleared, and open it last when enabling, as before. Mask the
slave interrupts in the handler whenever it sees one, not only when
interrupt processing is on: at probe and during a reset nothing handles
them, and mtk_sdw_enable_irq(true) unmasks them again. Rate-limit the
handler's slave status messages, so a storm from any other cause cannot
also flood the console.
---
 drivers/soundwire/mediatek/mtk-sdw-core.c | 59 ++++++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 39 insertions(+), 20 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soundwire/mediatek/mtk-sdw-core.c b/drivers/soundwire/mediatek/mtk-sdw-core.c
--- a/drivers/soundwire/mediatek/mtk-sdw-core.c
+++ b/drivers/soundwire/mediatek/mtk-sdw-core.c
@@ -692,6 +692,19 @@
 
 void mtk_sdw_enable_irq(struct mtk_sdw_core *core, bool enable)
 {
+	/*
+	 * Close the interrupt gate first when disabling. While it is open,
+	 * a slave interrupt that arrives once interrupt_enabled is false is
+	 * cleared but neither masked nor handed to the status work, and the
+	 * status work cancelled below re-enables the slave interrupts as it
+	 * finishes. A peripheral sitting at Dev0 raises its attach status
+	 * again as soon as it is cleared, so the window became an interrupt
+	 * storm on the CPU that had to finish the status work, the cancel
+	 * never returned and the gate stayed open for good.
+	 */
+	if (!enable)
+		mtk_sdw_update_intmask(core, MCP_INT_IRQ, 0);
+
 	if (enable) {
 		core_writel(core, MCP_SLAVEINTMASK0, MCP_SLAVE_INTMASK0_MASK);
 		core_writel(core, MCP_SLAVEINTMASK1, MCP_SLAVE_INTMASK1_MASK);
@@ -724,7 +737,8 @@
 		cancel_delayed_work_sync(&core->status_work);
 	}
 
-	mtk_sdw_update_intmask(core, MCP_INT_IRQ, enable ? MCP_INT_IRQ : 0);
+	if (enable)
+		mtk_sdw_update_intmask(core, MCP_INT_IRQ, MCP_INT_IRQ);
 }
 
 void mtk_sdw_enable_slave_irq(struct mtk_sdw_core *core, bool enable)
@@ -1152,37 +1166,42 @@
 
 	if (intstat & MCP_INT_SLV_MASK) {
 		if (intstat & MCP_INT_SLV_RESERVED) {
-			dev_info(core->dev, "[%u]slave reserved int IntStatus:0x%x\n",
-				 bus->link_id, intstat);
+			dev_info_ratelimited(core->dev,
+					     "[%u]slave reserved int IntStatus:0x%x\n",
+					     bus->link_id, intstat);
 		}
 
 		if (intstat & MCP_INT_SLV_ALERT) {
-			dev_info(core->dev,
-				 "[%u]slave alert int IntStatus:0x%x\n",
-				 bus->link_id, intstat);
+			dev_info_ratelimited(core->dev,
+					     "[%u]slave alert int IntStatus:0x%x\n",
+					     bus->link_id, intstat);
 		}
 
 		if (intstat & MCP_INT_SLV_ATTACHED) {
-			dev_info(core->dev,
-				 "[%u]slave attached int IntStatus:0x%x\n",
-				 bus->link_id, intstat);
+			dev_info_ratelimited(core->dev,
+					     "[%u]slave attached int IntStatus:0x%x\n",
+					     bus->link_id, intstat);
 		}
 
 		if (intstat & MCP_INT_SLV_NOT_ATTACH) {
-			dev_info(core->dev,
-				 "[%u]slave not attached int IntStatus:0x%x\n",
-				 bus->link_id, intstat);
+			dev_info_ratelimited(core->dev,
+					     "[%u]slave not attached int IntStatus:0x%x\n",
+					     bus->link_id, intstat);
 		}
+		/*
+		 * Mask before scheduling: the work re-enables the slave
+		 * interrupt when it finishes, so masking after
+		 * schedule_delayed_work() races a work that completes on
+		 * another CPU first and leaves the interrupt masked forever
+		 * (all subsequent attach/detach transitions are then lost).
+		 *
+		 * Mask while interrupt processing is off too (at probe, or
+		 * while a reset quiesces the link): nothing handles the
+		 * status then, and a peripheral at Dev0 raises it again as
+		 * fast as it is cleared. mtk_sdw_enable_irq(true) unmasks it.
+		 */
+		mtk_sdw_enable_slave_irq(core, false);
 		if (core->interrupt_enabled) {
-			/*
-			 * Mask before scheduling: the work re-enables the
-			 * slave interrupt when it finishes, so masking after
-			 * schedule_delayed_work() races a work that completes
-			 * on another CPU first and leaves the interrupt
-			 * masked forever (all subsequent attach/detach
-			 * transitions are then lost).
-			 */
-			mtk_sdw_enable_slave_irq(core, false);
 			schedule_delayed_work(&core->status_work, 0);
 			clearstat &= ~MCP_INT_SLV_MASK;
 		}
